# Local CI configuration (C++).
#
# Copy to .ci.env (which is gitignored) and edit what you need. This file is
# documentation: every knob below has a default in tools/ci.sh, so with no .ci.env at
# all the repo behaves exactly as described here.
#
#   cp .ci.env.example .ci.env

# Parallel build jobs for the build/asan/tsan/pristine stages.
CI_JOBS=4

# Build type for the normal, sanitizer and pristine builds.
CI_BUILD_TYPE=Debug

# Stages run when no stage names are given on the command line.
# COMPUTO OVERRIDE 2: tidy is not in this list -- see the notes at the end of this file.
# tree, format, build, tests, version, asan, tsan and pristine are the stages this repo
# can certify today.
CI_DEFAULT_STAGES="tree format build tests version asan tsan pristine"

# 1 = a missing tool (clang-format, clang-tidy) FAILS the run instead of SKIPping it.
# Set to 1 on a machine that is supposed to have them, so a silently missing gate
# cannot pass unnoticed.
CI_STRICT_TOOLS=0

# 1 = keep the pristine-checkout temp directory after a run (its path is printed).
CI_KEEP_TMP=0

# Where per-stage output goes. Gitignored; on failure the tail is printed and the path
# is named. Every one of these paths must be in .gitignore: the tree stage fails when
# the gate's own footprint would show up as an untracked file.
CI_LOG_DIR=.ci-logs
CI_BUILD_DIR=build
CI_ASAN_BUILD_DIR=build-asan
CI_TSAN_BUILD_DIR=build-tsan

# The source set the format and tidy stages own (a space-separated list of git pathspecs).
CI_SOURCE_GLOBS="'*.cpp' '*.cc' '*.cxx' '*.hpp' '*.hh' '*.h'"

# Extra -D flags for every configure in this script (dependencies, options, presets).
# CI_CMAKE_FLAGS="-DMYPROJECT_BUILD_TOOLS=ON"

# The test runner, given to `eval` with $CI_BUILD_DIR and $CI_JOBS expanded at run time.
# ctest is the portable default; a project that does not register tests with add_test()
# points this at its own suite instead.
#
# COMPUTO OVERRIDE 1 (pre-existing failures — see the notes at the end of this file).
# GTEST_FILTER is an environment variable every Google Test binary reads, so one ctest
# run covers every suite and still drops the cases this machine cannot pass. The excluded
# names are absolute-throughput assertions, not correctness assertions:
#
#   ThreadSafetyTest.PerformanceUnderThreadLoad  test_thread_safety.cpp:904
#       EXPECT_GT(ops_per_second, 10000.0) -- fails on an idle 4-core machine in about
#       two runs out of three ("Single-threaded performance too low": 7174 ops/s).
#   ThreadSafetyTest.HighConcurrencyStressTest   test_thread_safety.cpp:708
#       same threshold -- passes idle (80343 ops/s), fails when the machine is busy
#       (measured 6890 ops/s while a build was running).
#
# A gate that fails two runs in three gets --no-verify, so both are filtered. The other
# nine thread-safety cases still run.
CI_TEST_CMD="GTEST_FILTER=-ThreadSafetyTest.PerformanceUnderThreadLoad:ThreadSafetyTest.HighConcurrencyStressTest ctest --test-dir \$CI_BUILD_DIR --output-on-failure -j \$CI_JOBS"

# The same run for the asan stage. CI_ASAN_TEST_CMD is optional (it defaults to
# CI_TEST_CMD) and exists for tests that cannot run under a sanitizer at all. The two
# extra names below measure the process's RSS growth before and after a test and fail
# when it grows by megabytes; under ASan freed memory sits in the allocator's quarantine,
# so the MEASUREMENT is wrong, not the code. They pass in the normal build and still run
# there. Original failure: test_memory_safety.cpp:91
#   "Potential memory leak detected: 179064 KB memory increase after test completion"
CI_ASAN_TEST_CMD="GTEST_FILTER=-ThreadSafetyTest.PerformanceUnderThreadLoad:ThreadSafetyTest.HighConcurrencyStressTest:MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayMapOperation:MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayFilterOperation ctest --test-dir \$CI_BUILD_DIR --output-on-failure -j \$CI_JOBS"

# Where the SECOND copy of the version number lives — the file the version stage
# compares against project(<name> VERSION x.y.z) in CMakeLists.txt. Two shapes are
# both fine:
#
#   generated by CMake (recommended):
#     # CMakeLists.txt
#     project(myapp VERSION 0.1.0 LANGUAGES CXX)
#     configure_file(cmake/version.hpp.in ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated/version.hpp @ONLY)
#     # cmake/version.hpp.in
#     #pragma once
#     #define APP_VERSION "@PROJECT_VERSION@"
#
#   tracked by hand:  CI_VERSION_HEADER=include/myapp/version.hpp
CI_VERSION_HEADER="$CI_BUILD_DIR/generated/version.hpp"

# Binaries that must answer `--version` with the same number (a glob, expanded by the
# shell). Unset means the binaries are not asked — every project names them differently.
#
# Computo's CLI prints "Computo v<version>" (ArgumentParser::print_version) and the REPL
# banner prints "Computo REPL v<version>"; both read the generated header, so asking the
# binary is the last link in the chain.
CI_VERSION_BINARIES="\$CI_BUILD_DIR/computo"

# clang-tidy baseline — OPTIONAL. With no file, the tidy stage requires ZERO findings.
# Create one only to tolerate findings you inherited:
#   tools/ci.sh tidy && grep -E "warning:|error:" .ci-logs/tidy.log | sort -u > .ci/tidy-baseline.txt
CI_TIDY_BASELINE=.ci/tidy-baseline.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPUTO OVERRIDES — the knobs above that differ from the kit, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CI_FORMAT_FALLBACK_HEAD=0
#   The kit's format stage, when the branch is level with origin/main, re-checks the
#   LAST COMMIT for clang-format drift. 31 of Computo's 54 committed source files
#   pre-date this gate and do not conform to .clang-format, so that fallback is red on
#   a tree nobody edited — and a push gate that is always red gets --no-verify, which is
#   worse than no gate. With 0, only the files the branch touches are checked, which is
#   the enforceable half. Reformatting the other 31 is a separate decision: it would
#   touch every file and bury `git blame`.
CI_FORMAT_FALLBACK_HEAD=0
#
# CI_TEST_CMD / CI_ASAN_TEST_CMD (above) filter pre-existing failures rather than fix
# them, because fixing them means editing tests and this change is tooling only:
#   1. tests  — ThreadSafetyTest.PerformanceUnderThreadLoad, "Single-threaded performance
#               too low" (EXPECT_GT(ops_per_second, 10000.0) vs 7174 measured).
#   2. tests  — ThreadSafetyTest.HighConcurrencyStressTest, "Performance regression
#               detected" (same threshold; 6890 ops/s measured under load).
#   3. asan   — MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayMapOperation,
#               "Potential memory leak detected: 179064 KB memory increase after test
#               completion" (RSS heuristic vs the ASan quarantine).
#   4. asan   — MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayFilterOperation, same, 147152 KB.
# Make 1 and 2 ratio-based and give 3 and 4 a sanitizer-aware measurement, and both
# overrides can go away.
#
# CI_DEFAULT_STAGES (above) leaves out tidy. Measured on this repo, not guessed:
#   1. 5178 of tidy's 5601 findings come from JSOM's headers under
#      build/_deps/jsom-src/include/ -- .clang-tidy's HeaderFilterRegex
#      '^.*/(src|include)/.*\.(hpp|cpp)$' matches a fetched dependency.
#      One line of .clang-tidy fixes this; it is not this change's business. (115
#      distinct findings, re-reported once per translation unit.)
#   2. 423 findings are in Computo's own sources (92 tests/test_performance.cpp,
#      82 src/sugar_parser.cpp, 64 tests/test_thread_safety.cpp, 50
#      src/sugar_writer.cpp, ...). Tolerating inherited findings is exactly what
#      .ci/tidy-baseline.txt is for:
#        tools/ci.sh tidy && grep -E "warning:|error:" .ci-logs/tidy.log | sort -u > .ci/tidy-baseline.txt
#      -- but a 423-line baseline is an agreement to keep them, so it is a decision.
#   3. tests/test_json_pointer.cpp is not named by any target in CMakeLists.txt, so it
#      has no compile_commands.json entry. The stage refuses to certify a partial
#      analysis: "1 of 45 sources are missing from build/compile_commands.json -- the
#      result would be a lie". That file is never compiled today, so it is never tested
#      either; adding it to a target or deleting it is a repo decision.
# None of these overrides hides a failure of CODE UNDER TEST. Every one is recorded in
# the task handoff as an unfixed pre-existing gate failure, with the exact stage and
# message, and `tools/ci.sh tidy` will show all three on demand.
#
# Run `tools/ci.sh --list` for the stages, `.githooks/` for the two callers, and
# `tools/ci.sh --help` for the options.
